{"duration_api_ms":30899,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"c7bd7eb0-c118-42e7-a236-163b9340ee4e","total_cost_usd":0.17400500000000002,"usage":{"input_tokens":12,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17000,"cache_read_input_tokens":170085,"output_tokens":2747,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":617},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17000},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":12,"outputTokens":2747,"cacheReadInputTokens":170085,"cacheCreationInputTokens":17000,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.17400500000000002,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":617,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":7,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The fix is done and all tests pass, but I couldn't commit it: git has no user name or email configured here. The changes are staged.\n\n- **Change:** `recovery.go` now handles `http.ErrAbortHandler` (including wrapped copies) the same way it handles a broken pipe. It logs one short line with no stack trace, records the error on the context and aborts. It no longer calls the recovery handler, so no 500 gets written. Go's own HTTP server doesn't log this panic either. `httputil.ReverseProxy` raises it when the client disconnects.\n- **Test:** I added `TestPanicWithAbortHandler` in `recovery_test.go`. It checks that the response keeps its status (204, not 500), the custom handler isn't called, and no stack trace is logged.\n- **Why this approach:** it reuses the existing broken-pipe path, a 5-line change. The other option was to re-panic so Go's HTTP server drops the connection itself, but that would bypass gin's other middleware and change behaviour more.\n- **Checks:** the new test failed before the fix (500, handler called, stack trace logged) and passes after. When I put the old `recovery.go` back temporarily, it failed again. `go vet` and `gofmt` are clean, and `go test ./...` passes in full. The change is 30 added lines across 2 files.\n- **Behaviour change:** a custom recovery handler no longer sees `ErrAbortHandler` panics. This already happens for broken pipes.\n\nTo commit, set `git config user.name` and `git config user.email`, then run `git commit` (I didn't want to set an identity for you). My commit message was: `fix(recover): suppress http.ErrAbortHandler in recover`.","ttft_ms":3691,"type":"result","duration_ms":38026,"uuid":"97700e41-2892-4ec7-b13c-cfbfdc20f811","ttft_stream_ms":3010,"time_to_request_ms":94,"first_content_frame_ms":3010,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
